==== exit ====
0
==== stdout ====
raphael help content - Note bodies, files, stdin and Markdown conversion.

Create a note with `create resource.note`. Without --title, a note takes its
title from the text of the body's first paragraph or heading, with its line
breaks read as spaces, then from the description's first line, cut at 200
characters. A title must still be available when the body is empty.

Body text is literal unless it starts with @: `--body @draft.md` reads a file,
and `--body @-` reads stdin. Use --body-literal to keep a leading @ as text. A
body read from stdin or a file that is empty or only whitespace is refused. To
explicitly clear an existing body use update with `--body ''` and --revision. A
pipe is read only when `--body @-` is given; on a terminal, @- says how to
finish typing.

A value that starts with -, such as a Markdown list, must be joined to its flag
with =, as in `--body='- first item'`; otherwise it is read as another option
and refused. A longer body is easier as a file or a pipe: `--body @list.md` or
`--body @-`.

Metadata and filters accept inline JSON or @file, but not @-. Metadata is set
once at create and cannot be updated. File and stdin reads are bounded, and
invalid independent arguments are refused before reading body content.

Body formats: markdown, tiptap. Supported Markdown heading levels: 1, 2, 3.
Supported document elements: doc, paragraph, text, heading, bulletList,
orderedList, listItem, codeBlock, blockquote, horizontalRule, hardBreak.
Supported text styles: bold, italic, strike, code, link. Documents allow at most
10000 elements, nested at most 64 levels deep counting each JSON object and
array, so about half that many levels of elements. The complete serialized
request may contain at most 1048576 UTF-8 bytes.

Markdown the stored document cannot represent is not stored as written: a table
is kept as Markdown source in a code block; a task list checkbox is kept as
plain text; an image is kept as plain text; raw HTML is kept as plain text; a
link whose destination is not allowed is kept as plain text; a link with no
label is left out; a link title is left out; a footnote is kept as plain text; a
heading deeper than level 3 (#### or more) is kept as Markdown source in a code
block. When that happens, create and update warn on standard error and in
warnings[]; --strict refuses the body instead, with exit 2. Escaping, joined
lines and other rewrites that keep the meaning are not reported. Text such as
List<String> is an HTML tag by CommonMark's rules; put it in backticks to keep
it as code.

A body that is a TipTap JSON document must be sent with `--body-format tiptap`;
sent as Markdown it is refused. To keep JSON as note text, put it in a fenced
code block. Markdown output is a best-effort export of the stored document; use
`--json --return-format tiptap` to read its structured body. --return-format
requires --json; it controls the response, while --body-format controls
submitted content.

Examples:
  raphael create resource.note /work/idea --body '# An idea'
  raphael get /work/idea --json --return-format tiptap
  raphael update /work/idea --body '' --revision 3

Next: raphael help reference
